- `messages[:\s].{0,40}exceeds limit` hmm, variants: `messages: array length 2051 exceeds limit 2048` covered by the first. "too many messages" — add. "messages count exceeds" — add.

But these patterns go into `_overflowPatterns` (making isContextOverflow true) — good. And a separate parse function extracts M.

**Coalesce step**: merge adjacent tool-call/result pairs into compact messages. Simplest correct implementation: iterate the message list; group runs of (AssistantMessage with toolCalls followed by their ToolResultMessages) and replace each group older than the newest user message with a single user text message: `[coalesced N tool turns: <tool names> — results elided]`. Keep system prompt? The system prompt in this codebase is separate (`Context.systemPrompt`). "never drops the newest user message or the system prompt" — system prompt is separate field; but coalesce works on messages list. Keep all user messages except... hmm "only whole tool-call/result pairs older than the cut point go" — the cut point is the newest user message.

Implementation: find index of last UserMessage; everything before it that forms tool groups gets coalesced; also text-only old assistant messages can be merged into adjacent compact messages? Keep it focused: coalesce tool call/result pairs before the last user message into single user messages. Pairing invariant trivialy holds (we remove whole pairs and insert user text messages). Then run `repairToolPairing` on the result for safety? The result only contains standalone user messages and uncoalesced region which was pairing-valid... Actually the boundary between coalesced region and kept region: the kept region starts at/after the last user message; if the kept region begins with a tool result (orphan), repairToolPairing fixes it. Run repair at the end to guarantee invariants (AC6).

Target count: coalesce until under cap − margin, or everything droppable is coalesced. Merging pairs one group at a time reduces count by (groupSize - 1) per group. Group of assistant+result = 2 → 1. If cap is 2048 and count is 2059, coalescing alone can't realistically get under... each merge saves 1 message. Coalescing 11 groups saves 11. Hmm, that's not a "real reducer" — the ticket acknowledges: coalesce first (fast), then compaction pass (the real reducer). So the reactive repair: coalesce the outbound payload (helps marginally + makes room), then run a compaction pass over the live transcript via host relief hook (that reduces the transcript durably), rebuild context, retry once.

Wait, but the compaction pass reduces the transcript by summarizing — that happens via host `capRelief` hook (like `overWindowRelief`). After relief, the loop continues with the relieved context and rebuilds the request (which will include coalesce via `_buildRequestContext`? No — coalesce is part of the cap repair path, applied to the rebuilt request payload). Let me design the loop flow:

